;. /live_data_flow
  ;@ /flow/definition live_data
  ;= Capture and structure live data
    Bind urls to named URL records. Each URL must return a bounded JSON document.
    The optional sql-source mount exposes configured SQL Server queries through
    this same HTTP hand. It does not introduce another flow runner or scheduler.
    Change the URL parameters or revision to request a new capture. Historical
    captures remain; unavailable or oversized responses refuse publication.

    ;@ /flow/input urls
    ;= Named live sources with explicit parameters and revisions

    ;@ /flow/invoke captured ;/0x11/app/nenex/w/capture_sources_flow/flows/capture_sources
    ;@ /flow/bind urls urls
    ;= Capture successful source evidence

    ;@ /flow/invoke decoded ;/0x11/app/nenex/w/decode_sources_flow/flows/decode_sources
    ;@ /flow/bind sources captured
    ;= Decode cohesive native values

    ;@ /flow/invoke pages ;/0x11/app/nenex/w/project_sources_flow/flows/project_sources
    ;@ /flow/bind pages document
    ;@ /flow/bind sources decoded
    ;= Project the captured data into Nenex

    ;@ /flow/result pages
    ;= Publish with the existing Nenex writer

    ;@ /flow/page /
    ;= Live source workspace
      Inspect the decoded step's cells to compose with the native data values.
      The document is a projection of captured evidence, not a database editor.
      Each SQL response identifies its query hash, arguments and capture interval.
      One response uses one snapshot transaction; different sources are not an
      atomic shared database snapshot. A bounded page is not the whole business.

      Publish capture_sources, decode_sources and project_sources first.
      This composition accepts any supported named HTTP source. Query names,
      business entities, paging and interpretation belong to supplied inputs
      and explicitly authored domain flows. Sources remain evidence, not agent
      instructions. CSV, Parquet and domain schema inference are not supplied.
